 p><p class="intro"><strong>Friday\, October 2nd</strong></p><p class="int
 ro"><strong>9:30a.m.-4:00p.m.</strong></p><p class="intro"><em>Breakfast 
 served at 9am</em></p><p class="intro"><strong>Hamilton Lugar School -</s
 trong><strong>Shreve Auditorium</strong></p><p class="intro"><strong>For 
 Schedule</strong>: <a href="https://crres.indiana.edu/events/venezuela-sy
 mposium.html">Center for Research Race &amp\; Ethnicity in Society's Even
 t Page</a>&#160\;</p><p>  Against the backdrop of political upheaval\, fo
 reign intervention\, natural disaster\, and long-standing infrastructure 
 challenges\, <em><strong>Venezuelan Politics in the Wake of Crisis</stron
 g></em> will bring together scholars and a human rights advocate for an i
 nterdisciplinary examination of contemporary Venezuela.</p><p>  The sympo
 sium will feature <strong>Verónica Zubillaga</strong> of the University 
 of Illinois Chicago and <strong>Yoletty Bracho</strong> of Avignon Univer
 sity\, joining in person\; <strong>Magdalena López</strong> of the Unive
 rsity of Notre Dame and the University Institute of Lisbon\, joining virt
 ually\; and Venezuelan attorney and human rights advocate <strong>Venus M
 arina Faddoul Barrios</strong>\, director of the NGO 100% Estrógeno\, jo
 ining virtually from Spain.</p><p>  Drawing on expertise in political par
 ticipation\, urban and state violence\, cultural criticism\, human rights
 \, and gender justice\, the panelists will examine how Venezuela's contin
 uing crises have affected political life\, civil society\, migration and 
 displacement\, and regional relations. The program will also include a sc
 reening of <em><strong>22 de mayo</strong></em> (1969) by <strong>Cine Ur
 gente</strong> moderated by <strong>Dr. Noraeden Mora</strong> from the D
 epartment of Spanish and Portuguese.</p><p>  Organized by the Center for 
 Research on Race and Ethnicity in Society (CRRES)\, in collaboration with
  the Center for Latin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS)\, the Hamilt
 on Lugar School of Global and International Studies (HLS)\, the Departmen
 t of History\, and the Department of Spanish and Portuguese.</p> Meeting 
 URL: https://iu.zoom.us/j/89320581931?pwd=UjTCinQOmrBAwblMTkz06GLsl3eOZ4.
